# Why tip a waiter you will never see again?
voices: {"a":"gemini:Achird | curious, open-minded economist host who loves a puzzle; warm, thinks aloud, brisk lively pace (never slow, never rushed); takes objections seriously, never preachy","b":"gemini:Kore | sharp, friendly co-host: asks what a smart listener would, pushes back with real objections, sometimes explains; dry wit, never exaggerated; brisk lively pace, never slow"}
prompt: "Where do norms come from? Can economics explain them, or only include them?"
next: {"title":"Can rational choice explain crime?","href":"https://www.drawcast.app/#gh=hmelberg/drawcast-library/econ-from-zero/93-can-rational-choice-explain-crime.cast"}

## The tip nobody enforces
sources:
    - id: pew2023
      title: 'Tipping Culture in America: Public Sees a Changed Landscape'
      authors: Pew Research Center
      year: 2023
      url: https://www.pewresearch.org/2023/11/09/tipping-culture-in-america-public-sees-a-changed-landscape/
      finding: 77% of US adults say service quality is a major factor in whether and how much they tip; 92% always or often tip at sit-down restaurants.
    - id: lynn
      title: Should Tipping Be Banned? (Freakonomics Radio, interview with Michael Lynn)
      authors: Michael Lynn
      year: 2013
      finding: In the interview Lynn puts the average service–tip correlation at about 0.2, i.e. a few percent (about four) of the variation in tip percentages explained by service ratings.
    - id: azar2020
      title: The Economics of Tipping
      authors: Ofer H. Azar
      year: 2020
      doi: 10.1257/jep.34.2.215
      finding: Tipping amounts to tens of billions of dollars a year in the US and is a major source of income for millions of workers.
    - id: japan
      title: Etiquette in Japan (Wikipedia)
      authors: Wikipedia
      year: 2026
      finding: Tipping is not a custom in Japan; any gratuity is a fixed service charge or built into the price.
    - id: lynnmccall2000
      title: 'Gratitude and gratuity: a meta-analysis of research on the service-tipping relationship'
      authors: Michael Lynn & Michael McCall
      year: 2000
      doi: 10.1016/s1053-5357(00)00062-7
      finding: Across 13 studies (2,547 dining parties, 20 restaurants) tip size rises with service evaluations, but the relationship is much smaller than generally supposed (Journal of Socio-Economics 29:203–214).

