    card title "The tip nobody enforces"

Why leave money you don't owe, to a waiter in a town you'll never visit again?
    draw receipt

B: Twenty percent on a fifty-dollar (@draw r_ten@) meal is ten dollars, handed to a stranger after the food is already eaten.

And it adds up: Americans tip  (@draw billions@)tens of billions of dollars a year, and millions of servers live on it.

    pause 0.3

B: The usual answer is that a tip pays for good service. Seventy-seven  (@draw statcard@)percent of Americans say service is a major factor in what they tip.

    erase billions

B: Then tips should follow s (@draw svc@)ervice closely. I'd guess service explains half the difference between one table's tip and the next.

    ask "How much of the difference in tips between tables does the service explain? Click the line." on svc store g default "50" reveal false
        right: You said {g}. Now hold it against the studies.

Only a few (@draw few truth truth_cap@) percent. Michael Lynn, who has studied tipping for decades, finds the link between service ratings and tips positive but weak: service explains only a small slice of the variation.

    pause 0.5

B: So the tip barely moves with the service. Something else is setting it.
    highlight truth effect circle

    erase svc few truth truth_cap statcard

Self-interest has two more storie (@draw c1@)s. Maybe you're buying better service next time. But you're n (@draw x1@)ever coming back.

B: Or you're dodging the waiter's g (@draw c2@)lare. But you can pay by card and be out of the door  (@draw x2@)before anyone checks.

What's left is the nor (@draw c3@)m itself: ab (@highlight c3 effect pulse@)out twenty percent in America, no (@draw japan@)thing in Japan, where tipping isn't a custom.

B: That's a description, not an explanation. Where does 'what is do (@draw q@)ne' come from, and why does anyone obey it with nobody watching?

    pause 0.5

## A norm that holds itself up
use: equation_plot
with:
    equation: C(t) = t + gamma*max(0, E - t)
    variable: t
    params:
      gamma: {value: 0, min: 0, max: 2, step: 0.1, label: guilt γ}
      E: {value: 20, min: 0, max: 25, step: 1, label: expected tip E}
    controls: equation
    equation_form: both
    x_range:
      - 0
      - 35
    y_range:
      - 0
      - 50
    x_label: Tip, % of bill
    y_label: Cost to you
    marks:
      - {kind: point, at: 'E*min(1, max(0, floor(gamma)))', x_label: best tip, id: best}
sources:
    - id: cd2006
      title: Promises and Partnership
      authors: Gary Charness and Martin Dufwenberg
      year: 2006
      doi: 10.1111/j.1468-0262.2006.00719.x
      finding: 'Guilt aversion: people suffer guilt when they let down what others expect of them; promises raise those expectations and cooperation.'
    - id: bd2007
      title: Guilt in Games
      authors: Pierpaolo Battigalli and Martin Dufwenberg
      year: 2007
      finding: A formal model in which a player's utility falls with how far he lets down another's expectation.
    - id: japan
      title: Etiquette in Japan (Wikipedia)
      authors: Wikipedia
      year: 2026
      finding: Tipping is not a custom in Japan.

    text japan "Japan" #2f6b8f at.data [4,9] cites ["japan"] font_size 36 hidden true
    cards mathq options [{"text":"Show me the math","goto":"math"},{"text":"Skip it","goto":"test"}] then test hidden true
    scratch m center work ["Below E:  C = t + γ·(E − t)",{"tex":"\\text{slope} = 1 - \\gamma"},"γ > 1: slope < 0, so tip up to E",{"tex":"t^* = E \\text{ for any } E"}] font_size 36 hidden true

    card title "A norm that holds itself up"

If service barely moves the tip, what someone expects must be d (@draw axes curve_0@)oing the work. Start with the cost of tipping and nothing else.

B: Every point of tip is a point of the bill out of your pocket. On (@draw best@) this line alone, the cheapest tip is zero.

Now add guilt. Economists Gary Charness an (@draw equations@)d Martin Dufwenberg call it guilt aversion: it hurts to fall short of what someone expects of you.

    keep curve_0

Say the waiter expects twenty percent, and (@animate params.gamma 1.5 duration 3@) every point you leave short stings one and a half times as much as the money.

B: So the bottom of the curve sits at exactly twenty. Not because twenty is fair, but because it's expected.
    highlight best effect pulse

And why does the waiter expect twenty? Because (@highlight eq_param_E effect circle@) diners leave twenty. The expectation and the tip prop each other up.

    erase curve_0_ghost

    keep curve_0

B: Then twenty isn't special. Let th (@animate params.E 10 duration 2.5@)e waiter expect ten, and ten becomes the best tip.

And if nobody expe (@animate params.E 0 duration 2.5@)cts anything, leaving nothing costs nothing. That's Japan: the same mo (@draw japan@)del, a different equilibrium.

    erase japan curve_0_ghost

    animate params.E 20 duration 1.5

B: Try it: drag the guilt below one and the best tip collapses to zero; move the expectation and the best tip follows it.
    explore

    animate params.gamma 1.5 params.E 20 duration 1

So the model explains why a norm stays put once it exists: anyone who falls short pays in guilt. It can't say which number a place ends up with.
    highlight best effect pulse

    draw mathq

    ask "Show the math, or skip it?" on mathq stage own
        intro: The algebra takes four lines.

@math

    erase mathq

    fade axes curve_0 equations best to 0.15

Below the expectation, the cost is the tip plus guilt, gamma, times the shortfall.
    draw m_box m_line_1

So each extra point of tip changes the cost by one minus gamma.
    draw m_line_2

With gamma above one that slope is negative: tipping more makes you feel better than it costs, right up to E.
    draw m_line_3

And that works for any E at all. Many equilibria, one model.
    draw m_line_4

    erase m

@test

    erase mathq

    fade axes curve_0 equations best to 1

## Where do norms come from?
use: timeline
with:
    view:
      from: 1900
      to: 2030
    box:
      x: 40
      'y': 470
      w: 920
      h: 200
    eras:
      - {from: 1909, to: 1926, label: Tip bans}
    events:
      - {id: wa, date: 1909, label: Washington bans tips, priority: 1}
      - {id: rep, date: 1926, label: Last ban repealed, priority: 1}
      - {id: meyer, date: 2015, label: Meyer drops tips, sublabel: prices +21%, priority: 1}
      - {id: pew, date: 2023, label: '72%: tips expected in more places', priority: 1, emphasize: true}
sources:
    - id: segrave
      title: 'Tipping: An American Social History of Gratuities'
      authors: Kerry Segrave
      year: 1998
      finding: Six US states passed laws against tipping; the first in 1909 (Washington), the last repealed in 1926 (Mississippi).
    - id: meyer
      title: Danny Meyer (Wikipedia)
      authors: Wikipedia
      year: 2026
      finding: In 2015 Meyer announced he would eliminate tipping at all his restaurants; prices rose by about 21 percent.
    - id: pew2023
      title: 'Tipping Culture in America: Public Sees a Changed Landscape'
      authors: Pew Research Center
      year: 2023
      url: https://www.pewresearch.org/2023/11/09/tipping-culture-in-america-public-sees-a-changed-landscape/
      finding: 72% of US adults say tipping is expected in more places than five years ago.
    - id: elster
      title: Social Norms and Economic Theory
      authors: Jon Elster
      year: 1989
      doi: 10.1257/jep.3.4.99
      finding: Social norms are emotional and behavioural propensities, not outcome-oriented; they are sustained by approval and disapproval and by shame and guilt.
    - id: azar2004
      title: What sustains social norms and how they evolve? The case of tipping
      authors: Ofer H. Azar
      year: 2004
      finding: If a costly norm is sustained only by avoiding disapproval, it erodes over time; that tipping rates rose suggests people also gain from tipping (looking generous, self-image).
    - id: ellingsen
      title: Testing guilt aversion
      authors: Tore Ellingsen, Magnus Johannesson, Sigve Tjøtta and Gaute Torsvik
      year: 2010
      finding: 'When the recipient''s expectations were elicited and shown to the decision maker, giving barely responded to them: little support for guilt aversion.'

    scratch m work [{"tex":"t^* = E"},"E = ?"] x 260 y 270 font_size 40 hidden true
    text elster "Elster: included, not explained" #b5482e cites ["elster"] x 260 y 165 font_size 28 hidden true
    text az1 "Disapproval alone:\nnorm erodes" #3d3833 cites ["azar2004"] x 720 y 300 font_size 32 hidden true
    text az2 "Yet tips rose" #b5482e x 720 y 200 font_size 40 hidden true
    text s1a "Norms hold themselves up" #3d3833 x 330 y 425 font_size 34 hidden true
    text s1b "mostly agreed" #2f6b8f x 760 y 425 font_size 44 hidden true
    text s2a "Guilt is the glue" #3d3833 cites ["ellingsen"] x 330 y 360 font_size 34 hidden true
    text s2b "contested" #b5482e x 760 y 360 font_size 44 hidden true
    text s3a "Where norms come from" #3d3833 x 330 y 295 font_size 34 hidden true
    text s3b "open" #8a5fa8 x 760 y 295 font_size 44 hidden true
    group st1 members ["s1a","s1b"] hidden true
    group st2 members ["s2a","s2b"] hidden true
    group st3 members ["s3a","s3b"] hidden true

    card title "Where do norms come from?"

B: So the model holds a norm in pla (@draw axis@)ce but can't pick it. History picks, and the American tip had a rough one.

Tipping was once fought in America. Six stat (@draw era_0 event_wa@)es passed laws against it, the first of them Washington, in 1909.

B: By 1926 the  (@draw event_rep@)last of those laws was repealed. The norm had won.

And it keeps moving. In 2023, seventy-two percent of American (@draw event_pew@)s told Pew that tipping is expected in more places than five years before.

B: Here's my objection. Jon Elster argued that norms aren't tools for an outcom (@draw m_box m_line_1@)e; they're held up by shame and guilt. Put guilt into a c (@draw m_line_2@)ost curve and you've included (@draw elster@) the norm, not explained it.

Ofer Azar adds a puzzle: if people tipped (@draw az1@) only to dodge disapproval, the norm should slowly erode. In (@draw az2@)stead American tips have crept up.

B: So tipping must also pay in something else: looking generous, or feeling generous.
    highlight event_pew

    erase m elster az1 az2

How sure are we? That a norm can hold itself up thro (@draw st1@)ugh what people expect of each other: mostly agreed.

B: That guilt is the gl (@draw st2@)ue: contested. When researchers told people exactly what the other side expected, the link largely disappeared.

And where norms come fr (@draw st3@)om, and why they shift: still open.

    fade axis events eras to 0.2

    quiz "Mia's bakery in Harbourtown adds a card screen suggesting 10, 15 or 20 percent. In the expectation model, why might people who never tipped for bread start?" intro "Three new cases."
        right: The screen announces that a tip is now expected. Once you think someone expects one, leaving nothing starts to sting.
        wrong: In the model, what makes leaving nothing costly? Here: the screen tells you a tip is expected.
        + The screen announces that a tip is now expected
        * The bread got better
        * They want better bread next week

    quiz "In 2015 Danny Meyer dropped tipping at his New York restaurants and raised menu prices by about a fifth. What does the norm model warn about going it alone?" voice b
        right: His menus look dearer. Every rival's price still leaves the tip out, so one restaurant can't leave the norm alone without looking expensive.
        wrong: Diners compare menu prices, and everyone else's prices exclude a tip. Here: his menus look dearer.
        + His menus look dearer than rivals' that leave the tip out
        * Diners will tip on top anyway
        * Nothing: diners pay the same in total

    draw event_meyer

    quiz "Food-delivery apps arrived with no tipping custom of their own. In the expectation model, what sets the tip there?"
        right: Whatever the default makes people expect: the suggested tip tells you what the courier expects, and falling short of it stings.
        wrong: A norm lives in expectations, and a new market has none until something sets them: here, the app's default suggestion.
        + Whatever the app's default suggestion makes people expect
        * The quality of the delivery
        * Nothing: nobody tips a stranger they never see

So why tip a waiter you'll never see again? Because a norm says so, and keeping it feels better than breaking it, whether that's g (@highlight st1 effect underline@)uilt, pride or habit. Economics can show why a norm holds; which number (@highlight event_wa event_pew@) a country lands on is its history.
    fade axis events eras to 1

    pause 0.5

## Where next
